/* SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
   SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

   The application's design tokens. Start here when you want it to look different.

   ⚠ **The name is the convention and the whole of the configuration.** The build step globs for a
   file called vixen.ui.vcss anywhere under the project, finds this, and every class name in every
   .vxml and every .cs in the project resolves against it. One per project — two would be two
   palettes, which is the thing a token model exists to prevent, so the build refuses rather than
   picking.

   ⚠ **The glob is described rather than written out, and that is not fastidiousness about prose.**
   CSS comments do not nest and they have no escape: the first star-slash in a comment closes it, so
   the pattern spelled literally would end this comment on its own second line and feed the
   paragraph you are reading to the parser as CSS. That happened, in the template this file is
   modelled on — "the", "to" and "is" were compiled as type selectors for as long as it was written
   that way.

   ⚠ **A project with no theme file still has tokens.** Everything below layers over the palette the
   engine ships — Tailwind v4's own twenty-six ramps in oklch — so bg-slate-800 works in a project
   that wrote nothing. `--color-*: initial;` is how an application that wants none of them says so,
   and `--color-blue-500: initial;` removes exactly one.

   A token is a custom property, so declaring --color-brand does two things at once: it makes
   var(--color-brand) available to hand-written CSS, and it tells the generator that bg-brand,
   text-brand and border-brand exist. */

@theme {
    /* 4px, which makes p-2 eight and p-4 sixteen and everybody able to see that one is twice the
       other. It is also the engine's default, so this line is a statement rather than a change —
       and it is the one to edit when the whole interface should breathe more. */
    --spacing: 4px;

    /* ⚠ The two colours this sample calls its own, and they are named after what they *are* rather
       than after what they look like. `--color-brand` renamed to teal is one line here; every
       `text-brand` in the markup keeps meaning "the accent", which is the argument that made a
       token model win over writing the hex where it is used. */
    --color-brand: oklch(74% 0.13 195);
    --color-brand-dim: oklch(58% 0.10 195);

    /* The chrome. Deliberately a *warm* dark rather than a neutral one: a tool window that sits next
       to a viewport reads as a hole cut in the screen when it is pure grey. */
    --color-chrome: #16181d;
    --color-chrome-raised: #1f2229;

    --radius-sm: 2px;
    --radius: 4px;
    --radius-md: 6px;
    --radius-lg: 10px;

    --text-xs: 11px;
    --text-xs--line-height: 15px;
    --text-sm: 12px;
    --text-sm--line-height: 16px;
    --text-base: 13px;
    --text-base--line-height: 19px;
    --text-lg: 16px;
    --text-lg--line-height: 22px;

    --font-weight-normal: 400;
    --font-weight-medium: 500;
    --font-weight-semibold: 600;

    /* A shadow is a designed thing: its offset, blur and alpha are chosen together to read as one
       height above the surface, which is why a token is a whole declaration rather than numbers to
       assemble at the use site. */
    --shadow-panel: 0 2px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45);
}
